<%= CsuiteFinderWeb.Layout.head(
      title: "Work emails for your whole pipeline — CSuiteFinder",
      description: "$#{@seat_usd} a month per seat, and every month that seat carries $#{@seat_usd} of lookup credit — around #{@seat_emails} verified work email addresses. Try it for $#{@trial_price}.",
      nav: :teams,
      audience: :sales,
      css: CsuiteFinderWeb.Layout.page_css(:teams)
    ) %>
<header>
  <h1>Your reps know who to call.<br>They just don't have the email.</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Give a name and a company, get a work email that has been checked against the
    live mailbox before you send. One seat is
    <strong>$<%= @seat_usd %> a month</strong> and carries
    $<%= @seat_usd %> of lookup credit every month — about
    <strong><%= @seat_emails %> verified work emails</strong>.
  </p>
  <div class="hero-cta">
    <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/checkout?plan=seat">Get a seat — $<%= @seat_usd %>/month</a>
    <a class="btn btn-ghost" href="/checkout">Try it first — $<%= @trial_price %></a>
  </div>
  <p class="cta-note">
    No contract, no call, no seat minimum. Cancel any time and the month you have
    paid for runs to its end.
  </p>
</header>

<%= CsuiteFinderWeb.Layout.sheet(
      rows: @sheet,
      rows_label: @sheet_rows,
      date: @sheet_date,
      visitor: @visitor
    ) %>

<h2 class="section-label">Build a bigger Rolodex, today</h2>

<div class="rolodex-band">
  <%!-- Drawn rather than photographed: the cards are generated from one arc, so
        the fan stays even and the whole thing is a few hundred bytes that
        recolour themselves in dark mode. --%>
  <%!-- Framed to the drawing rather than to a round number: at 60 degrees the
        outer cards and the base swing past a 560x300 box and get clipped. --%>
  <svg class="rolodex" viewBox="50 38 460 290" role="img"
       aria-label="A rolodex with its cards fanned open, each holding a name and a work email.">
    <%= for {angle, i} <- Enum.with_index(-60..60//15) do %>
      <g transform="rotate(<%= angle %> 280 268)">
        <rect x="212" y="66" width="136" height="176" rx="7"
              class="rolo-card <%= if i == 4, do: "front" %>"/>
        <rect x="272" y="60" width="16" height="17" rx="3" class="rolo-notch"/>
        <rect x="228" y="104" width="<%= 74 + rem(i * 17, 30) %>" height="8" rx="4" class="rolo-line strong"/>
        <rect x="228" y="124" width="<%= 92 - rem(i * 13, 26) %>" height="6" rx="3" class="rolo-line"/>
        <rect x="228" y="142" width="<%= 84 - rem(i * 9, 22) %>" height="6" rx="3" class="rolo-line"/>
        <%= if i == 4 do %>
          <circle cx="234" cy="176" r="9" class="rolo-tick-bg"/>
          <path d="M229.5 176.5l3.4 3.4 6-6.6" class="rolo-tick"/>
          <rect x="250" y="171" width="58" height="7" rx="3.5" class="rolo-line strong"/>
        <% end %>
      </g>
    <% end %>

    <%!-- The spindle and rails, drawn last so the cards sit behind them. --%>
    <path d="M92 252 q188 46 376 0" class="rolo-rail"/>
    <path d="M92 252 q188 46 376 0" class="rolo-rail inner"/>
    <rect x="86" y="240" width="18" height="40" rx="6" class="rolo-post"/>
    <rect x="456" y="240" width="18" height="40" rx="6" class="rolo-post"/>
    <circle cx="280" cy="268" r="10" class="rolo-hub"/>
  </svg>

  <div class="rolodex-copy">
    <p class="rolodex-lead">
      A rep's contact list is the whole job, and it stops growing the moment they
      run out of addresses.
    </p>
    <p class="quiet">
      One seat adds up to <strong><%= @seat_emails %> verified work emails</strong>
      a month — every one of them checked against the live mailbox before it
      reaches your sequence. You bring the names; the addresses stop being the
      part that slows everyone down.
    </p>
    <p style="margin:18px 0 0">
      <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/checkout?plan=seat">Get a seat — $<%= @seat_usd %>/month</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</div>

<h2 class="section-label">What a seat actually buys</h2>

<div class="numbers">
  <div class="num">
    <span class="n"><%= @seat_emails %></span>
    <span class="k">verified work emails a month</span>
  </div>
  <div class="num">
    <span class="n">Included</span>
    <span class="k">mailbox checks, job titles, company data</span>
  </div>
  <div class="num">
    <span class="n">$0</span>
    <span class="k">for a lookup that finds nothing</span>
  </div>
</div>

<p class="quiet">
  That is the whole pitch, and it is arithmetic rather than a claim:
  $<%= @seat_usd %> of lookup credit a month, and a seat spends it on work emails.
  Deliverability checks, company data, job titles and email formats are included
  — they draw nothing from the credit. You are charged for answers, so a name we
  cannot resolve costs you nothing.
</p>

<h2 class="section-label">The objections, answered plainly</h2>

<div class="faq">
  <div class="q">
    <strong>Will these bounce?</strong>
    <span>
      Every address can be checked against the live mailbox before you send, and
      that check is included. Two of the rows in the sheet above came back
      undeliverable — that is the point of showing them. A bounce you catch here
      costs nothing; one you catch after sending costs your sending domain.
    </span>
  </div>
  <div class="q">
    <strong>What if my reps won't learn another tool?</strong>
    <span>
      There is nothing to learn. They ask their assistant — ChatGPT, Claude,
      whatever they already use — in plain English, and it does the lookups.
      <a href="/start">One line of setup</a>, once.
    </span>
  </div>
  <div class="q">
    <strong>What happens to unused credit?</strong>
    <span>
      It does not roll over. Each month starts at $<%= @seat_usd %> again, which
      is why a seat is priced as a month of capacity rather than a balance.
      Credit you buy outright is separate and never expires.
    </span>
  </div>
  <div class="q">
    <strong>What if it doesn't work for my list?</strong>
    <span>
      Spend $<%= @trial_price %> and find out on your own names before you commit
      to anything. Same lookups, same terms — it is a trial of the seat rather
      than a different product.
      <strong>The credit expires after <%= @trial_months %> month</strong>, the
      same way a seat's does.
    </span>
  </div>
  <div class="q">
    <strong>Am I locked in?</strong>
    <span>
      No contract and no minimum. Cancel from your account page any time and the
      month you have already paid for runs to its end.
    </span>
  </div>
  <div class="q">
    <strong>Who is this for?</strong>
    <span>
      Teams that already know who they want to reach — founders selling into
      named accounts, reps working a territory list, recruiters chasing named
      candidates. It resolves people you can name. It is not a prospect database
      and will not build the list for you.
    </span>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- ── pricing ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     At the foot of the page on purpose: the nav's Pricing link is a bare
     #pricing fragment, so it scrolls down this page rather than sending a
     salesperson to the developer plan. -->

<h2 class="section-label" id="pricing">Pricing</h2>

<div class="plan">
  <div>
    <span class="price">$<%= @seat_usd %></span><span class="per">per person, per month</span>
  </div>
  <p class="gets">
    Every seat gets nearly <strong><%= @seat_emails %> corporate emails</strong> each month.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <%= for item <- @seat_includes do %>
    <li><%= item %></li>
    <% end %>
  </ul>
  <ul class="caveats">
    <%= for item <- @seat_caveats do %>
    <li><%= item %></li>
    <% end %>
  </ul>
  <p style="margin:24px 0 0">
    <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/checkout?plan=seat">Get a seat — $<%= @seat_usd %>/month</a>
  </p>
  <p class="cta-note" style="margin:14px 0 0">
    Rather try it on your own list first?
    <a href="/checkout">Try it for $<%= @trial_price %> →</a>
  </p>
</div>

<%= CsuiteFinderWeb.Layout.footer(:sales) %>

</div>
</body>
</html>
